WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:02.720
US national debt held by the public

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:07.047
is at almost $22 trillion or about $67,000

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.260
per citizen surpassing
the country's annual GDP

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:14.540
for the first time, since World War II.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:17.030
The congressional budget office
predicts that it'll reach

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:20.950
102% of GDP by the end of 2021

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:25.030
and hit 202% by 2051.

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:27.540
The federal government's growing
debt burden would increase

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:31.330
the risk of a fiscal crisis
and higher inflation as well as

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:33.740
undermine confidence in the US dollar.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:38.740
CBO concluded in it's March,
2021 long-term budget outlook.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.520
If the world were to lose
confidence in the dollar,

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:42.720
what could replace it?

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.860
Another fiat currency, gold, Bitcoin?

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:49.910
That was the topic of a recent
Oxford style debate hosted by

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:51.130
the Soho Forum.

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:53.130
John Vallis, a financial consultant

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.990
and host of the Bitcoin Rapid Fire podcast

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:58.610
believes that Bitcoin
will eventually replace

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:02.060
government's fiat money as the
preferred medium of exchange.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:04.660
He argues that Bitcoin's global adoption

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:06.493
is a matter of when, not if.

00:01:06.493 --> 00:01:09.370
Lawrence H. White an
economics professor at

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:11.860
George Mason University is skeptical

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:13.640
of Bitcoin's future as money.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.110
He believes that may have a
future as a financial asset,

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:20.800
but isn't suitable to become
a global medium of exchange.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:23.019
The debate was moderated
by Soho Forum, director,

00:01:23.019 --> 00:01:26.380
Gene Epstein, and held
before a live audience

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:28.340
at the Porcupine Freedom Festival,

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.670
better known as PorcFest in
Lancaster, New Hampshire.

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:35.290
Here is the Soho Forum debate
on the future of Bitcoin

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:38.320
as the world's medium of exchange.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:42.957
The resolution reads 'Bitcoin
will eventually replace

00:01:42.957 --> 00:01:45.281
government's fiat money

00:01:45.281 --> 00:01:47.270
as the preferred medium of exchange.'

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:50.350
Please vote, we'd like to
have a substantial vote

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:54.440
again you see it's yes, no, or undecided.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:57.580
And the program that was
specially written for us,

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:00.540
will make sure that your vote counts

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:02.800
only if you vote both times.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.340
And we can actually
trace the flow of voting

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.340
through the program app.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:10.490
So it was kind of a
fascinating exercise to do so,

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:12.620
but again, that's the resolution,

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.250
please vote yes, no or undecided.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:20.250
And to defend the resolution,
we have John Vallis,

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:23.120
who does the podcast, Bitcoin Fire.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:26.287
John Vallis, please come to the stage.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:30.970
Taking the negative on the resolution,

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:35.280
we have George Mason University
professor Larry White.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.947
Larry, please come to the stage.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:49.200
Jane, please close the voting, all closed.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:53.566
Thank you, Jane, and
John, you have 15 minutes

00:02:53.566 --> 00:02:56.893
to defend the resolution,
take it away, John.

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:03.503
Well, first of all,

00:03:03.503 --> 00:03:07.230
it's great to be here because
I'm actually a refugee.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:08.290
I may not look like it,

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:11.430
but I recently escaped
the Canadian gulags,

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:12.548
and now I'm here

00:03:12.548 --> 00:03:13.381
(audience cheers)

00:03:13.381 --> 00:03:14.630
in the land of the free,

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:17.150
and if my voice is a
little hoarse forgive me,

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:20.270
I was in Miami for the Bitcoin
conference, and since then,

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:22.493
I've been having a ton of
conversations with Bitcoiners down

00:03:22.493 --> 00:03:24.800
in Miami, and it's been amazing.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:27.720
So if I'm coughing a
little bit, I apologize.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:32.270
Also, I drove up from Boston
yesterday and I noticed on the

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:37.110
welcome to New Hampshire sign,
it said, live free or die.

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:38.700
And you got to imagine as a Canadian,

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:40.100
I'm not used to that kind of shit, right?

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:43.400
We're more used to like do
whatever the government tells you

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.720
to do and everything will be fine.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.395
So I thought that was
pretty fucking awesome

00:03:47.395 --> 00:03:49.053
to see that as a slogan.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:54.780
We have to keep this narrow
because this is the proposition

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:58.390
of Bitcoin will inevitably
replace government fiat as a

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:00.100
preferred medium of exchange.

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:01.310
Bitcoin is a very deep rabbit hole,

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:03.420
there's lots of stuff to discuss about it.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:05.420
So I'm gonna have to
necessarily keep a lot of this

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:09.228
fairly brief and I'll focus on
the resolution specifically.

00:04:09.228 --> 00:04:12.150
I'm not gonna be talking
about Austrian economics today

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:14.670
or quoting dead Austrians

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:17.673
or any kinds of economic
economists actually.

00:04:17.673 --> 00:04:21.000
And that's because I don't
think it's necessary.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.130
Bitcoin is money and money is something

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:25.770
we interact with on a daily basis

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:28.670
and so I think money is
a very intuitive thing.

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:30.330
It's just not something that people

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:33.990
have had the opportunity to
actually think about because

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:36.110
we've never really had a choice.

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:39.500
You use the government fiat or else,

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:41.640
but now we do have a
choice, and what this is

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:45.260
fostering is a tremendous
explosion of people that are for

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:47.010
the first time, thinking about money

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:48.620
and asking these questions about what

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:51.120
makes a good money and
what money should be

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:52.650
and what money shouldn't be.

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:55.500
And so what I have done
for my own Bitcoin journey

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.153
is to do that, to think
about this critically.

